1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:01,160
Welcome to the Deep Dive.

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:08,600
Today we're doing a, uh, a really close look into the hidden architecture of digital government services.

3
00:00:08,940 --> 00:00:11,760
Specifically, we're unpacking the service workflow.

4
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:14,920
Right. And our mission here is to understand how

5
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:23,460
mapping this all out prevents the systems in your ministry from becoming, you know, fragmented, siloed, duplicated, or just extremely costly to change.

6
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:28,260
Exactly. Because whether you're managing a license or an application in your program,

7
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:30,500
each case passes through stages.

8
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:33,940
And if those stages aren't written down once centrally...

9
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:37,620
Then every individual screen basically invents its own rules.

10
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:38,020
Right. I mean,

11
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:42,500
the law ends up being enforced on some screens, but completely ignored on others.

12
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:45,000
Which is incredibly risky for any government official.

13
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,080
So to prevent that, a functional service workflow has to be written once for the whole application,

14
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:55,760
and it explicitly defines three things. Number one,

15
00:00:56,440 --> 00:00:56,780
states.

16
00:00:57,820 --> 00:01:02,020
Yeah, states. So that is every stage a case can possibly be in.

17
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:03,300
Number two,

18
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:07,060
moves, which is every step from one state to another.

19
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:14,960
Exactly. And number three is who, the specific person who may make each move, and, uh, on what decision.

20
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:18,720
But wait, I have a question on that. If a move isn't written down in this

21
00:01:19,660 --> 00:01:20,520
central workflow,

22
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:24,780
does it mean it literally, like physically cannot happen?

23
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:27,480
Absolutely. Unwritten moves cannot happen.

24
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:33,280
Every screen will then only offer the moves the workflow allows and strictly to the people it names.

25
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:34,300
Wow.

26
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:37,920
Okay, so there are no workarounds. No workarounds.

27
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:41,900
And we see this structure handled really well in the GovStack specifications, right?

28
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:46,440
Yes, exactly. The GovStack workflow specification handles the broader process.

29
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:50,780
It describes a process as linked activities done by a person or a machine.

30
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:58,480
Okay. It basically asks a workflow block to run a process, and that could be started by another system, a click, or even just the passage of time.

31
00:01:58,740 --> 00:01:59,160
Got it.

32
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:02,080
And then you have the registration specification.

33
00:02:02,180 --> 00:02:04,600
Right, which is much more specific about human decisions.

34
00:02:05,060 --> 00:02:08,479
It gives an officer exactly three decisions on an application.

35
00:02:08,860 --> 00:02:10,860
Number one, approve. Number two, reject.

36
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:13,560
Or number three, send back for correction.

37
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:16,240
Okay, no making up rules on the fly. Exactly.

38
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:22,060
So to really see this in action, let's look at Progressa, which is the fictional country of these examples.

39
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:27,320
Specifically, we're looking at their Higher Education Quality Authority, PHEQA.

40
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:34,680
Right. So let's apply the states. For a PHEQA institution license, there are three specific states.

41
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:38,360
Number one, granted, meaning the institution may operate.

42
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:43,520
Okay. Number two, suspended, meaning it may not admit new students until the suspension ends.

43
00:02:43,960 --> 00:02:47,800
And number three, canceled, where it may no longer operate under this license.

44
00:02:48,140 --> 00:02:51,780
But who actually pulls the levers to move a license between those states?

45
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:54,260
Well, every move rests on a legal decision.

46
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:57,960
The minister decides to grant a license and to suspend or cancel it.

47
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:02,120
The minister also ends a suspension on PHEQA's advice.

48
00:03:02,620 --> 00:03:04,320
And what about the registration officer?

49
00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:06,920
The registration officer just records each decision.

50
00:03:06,980 --> 00:03:13,160
They don't make them. And crucially, a cancellation is undone only by a decision on appeal or on review that sets it aside.

51
00:03:13,520 --> 00:03:17,060
Oh, interesting. Which actually brings up the dead-end check.

52
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:22,080
This is a critical system test you must ask: Can every state be left?

53
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:24,140
Yes, you always have to ask that.

54
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:26,200
But wait, let me push back on that.

55
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:32,120
Looking at the Progressa example, wouldn't canceled inherently be a permanent dead end?

56
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:42,760
I mean, it definitely looks like one. But no, because an institution can appeal or a person can ask the minister to review, a set aside cancellation returns the license to

57
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:44,460
the state it held before.

58
00:03:44,660 --> 00:03:45,300
Ah, I see.

59
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:49,680
Therefore, no state is a true dead end, and the workflow shows the way out.

60
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:56,740
That makes total sense. Mm-hmm. So the ultimate takeaway for you listening right now is to ask for your service workflow on a single page.

61
00:03:56,800 --> 00:04:01,320
Yes. You need to see every state, every move, and who may make each move.

62
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:03,460
And check each move against the law,

63
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:06,340
and obviously look for dead ends. Exactly.

64
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:08,820
So we'll leave you with this thought to mull over.

65
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:12,760
If you were to map out a critical process in your program today,

66
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:19,040
how many unintentional dead ends might you discover simply because the workflow wasn't properly defined from the start?
